<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vastupala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1240</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vastupala was a Jain minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghela</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viradhavala">Viradhavala</a> at Dholka with his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tejapala">Tejapala</a> from about 1220. A capable administrator and general, he also defended Gujarat against outside attack. A poet himself, he patronised scholars and writers and built or restored many temples, notably on the sacred hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatrunjaya">Shatrunjaya</a>.</p></div>
